**Overloading vs overriding.**
- **Overloading:** several methods in the same scope with the **same name but different parameter lists** (count, types or order). The compiler picks one at **compile time** (static polymorphism). Return type alone cannot distinguish overloads.
- **Overriding:** a derived class provides its own implementation of a base-class method with the **same signature**. The method is chosen at **run time** from the actual object type (dynamic polymorphism). In C++ the base method must be `virtual`; in Java instance methods are virtual by default. The access level cannot be reduced, and `final` methods cannot be overridden.

```
class Shape { public: virtual double area() const { return 0; } };
class Circle : public Shape {
  double r;
public:
  double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; }
};
```

**Abstraction.** Showing only what an object does and hiding how. You expose a simple interface and keep details private, so users do not depend on the implementation and you can change it freely. Achieved by **abstract classes** (a class with at least one pure virtual function in C++, `abstract` in Java) and **interfaces**, and also through encapsulation (private data with public methods). Example: you call `list.sort()` without knowing the algorithm. Abstraction is a design-level idea; encapsulation is the mechanism of bundling data and restricting access.

**Static members.**
- A **static variable** belongs to the **class**, not to any object: one shared copy no matter how many objects exist (for example an object counter). It is stored in static storage, not inside each object.
- A **static method** can be called without an instance (`ClassName::method()`), and it has no `this` pointer, so it can only use static members directly. It cannot be virtual or overridden in C++ (in Java it can be hidden, not overridden).
- In C, `static` on a local variable keeps its value between calls, and on a global restricts it to the file.

**Interview tip.** Give one-line definitions first, then one tiny example each, and say when you would pick each (for example, static for utility functions and constants).
